target: internal/record/record.go
verify: go test ./internal/record/ -count=1 -run 'TestConvergenceRules'
expect: a_cycle_with_no_applied_field_is_not_converged
why:    the Python compares `last.get("applied") == 0`, and `None == 0` is False, so a MISSING or null applied does NOT satisfy the converged branch — it falls through to cap/halted. Routing it through num(), which maps absent and null to 0, derived `converged` where the Python derives `halted`: a clean result with no disclosure, bought by leaving a field out. That is the same hole the unresolved_asks check above exists to close, reopened one line below it. Re-anchored for the typed port: the rule is a pointer test now rather than isZero(), and the mutation makes a nil Applied read as zero — the same defect, one layer down. Typing makes the absent/zero distinction visible but not automatic: `Applied != nil && *Applied == 0` and `Applied == nil || *Applied == 0` are two operator substitutions apart (`!=`->`==`, `&&`->`||`).
--- old
	if last.Applied != nil && *last.Applied == 0 &&
		(last.Asked == nil || *last.Asked == 0) &&
		(last.AnalysisChanged == nil || !*last.AnalysisChanged) {
--- new
	if (last.Applied == nil || *last.Applied == 0) &&
		(last.Asked == nil || *last.Asked == 0) &&
		(last.AnalysisChanged == nil || !*last.AnalysisChanged) {
